# The comparison of Stacks 0BQY at a Koszul-regular ideal sheaf, locally

`Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.isIso_extComparison_forall` proves that composition makes

`⋀ⁱ_{O_X}(Ext¹_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)) ⟶ Extⁱ_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)`

an isomorphism in every degree, for a Koszul chain datum whose augmentation is a
quasi-isomorphism. That hypothesis is stated for an abstract datum; this file supplies the
canonical one and reaches the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 0BQY] itself.

Two steps. The constructed Koszul chain datum `Stacks.RegularSequence.koszulChainData` of a
morphism `φ : (O♯)ʳ ⟶ O♯` is a datum of the required shape, and
`Stacks.RegularSequence.koszulAugmentation` is by definition its augmentation, so the existing
acyclicity bridges discharge the quasi-isomorphism hypothesis verbatim. And a Koszul-regular
sheaf of ideals in the sense of Stacks tag 063D part (2) is, near every point of the support of
the quotient, generated by a sequence of sections whose Koszul complex is acyclic in positive
degrees, which `RegularIdealSheaf.exists_quasiIso_koszulAugmentation_of_isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf`
(in the namespace `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry`) turns into exactly that quasi-isomorphism on the
restricted ringed space.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RegularSequence.isIso_extComparison_koszulChainData`: the comparison is an isomorphism
  in every degree, for the canonical Koszul chain datum of a morphism whose Koszul complex
  resolves the quotient.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.isExteriorAlgebra_extSelfAlgebra_koszulChainData`: equivalently, the
  self-Ext algebra is an exterior algebra on its degree-one piece.
* `RegularIdealSheaf.forall_isIso_extComparison_of_isKoszulSequenceExactOn` (in the namespace
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry`): **the local statement at the hypothesis of the tag** — on an open
  `U` over which the ideal is generated by sections whose Koszul complex is acyclic in positive
  degrees, the comparison of the Koszul model is an isomorphism in every degree on the restricted
  ringed space `X|_U`. Since a Koszul-regular sheaf of ideals satisfies that condition near every
  point of the support of the quotient, this is the conclusion of the tag on each member of a
  cover.

## Implementation notes

**What is proved here is the tag's local step; the declarations below are not registered under
[Stacks, Tag 0BQY].** The tag asserts a single isomorphism of sheaves on
`Y`, formed from the comparison that composition on `RSheafHom_{O_Y}(O_X, O_X)` induces
intrinsically; what is proved here is that statement on each member of a cover, for the
comparison of a Koszul model on that member.

**The tag's single statement on `Y` is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isIso_desc_app_isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulSelfExtKoszulRegular`.** It takes the
self-Ext algebra in its model-free form — the composition-product algebra of a K-injective
complex presenting `O_X`, descended along the quotient map so as to be an algebra over `O_X` —
and glues the local statements below over the cover of opens carrying a Koszul presentation. The
paragraphs that follow describe the links of that gluing one at a time.

**The gluing.** The
local-to-global step itself is
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.isIso_desc_app_of_coversTop` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ExteriorAlgebraSliceRestriction`, with
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.isIso_app_of_coversTop` as its primitive and
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.SquareZero.of_coversTop` gluing the one hypothesis the comparison
map needs in order to exist at all; for a morphism of sheaves of modules the conservativity
statement wanted is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_of_isIso_overFunctor_map`, the paragraph above
naming `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QcohRestrictEquiv.isIso_of_isIso_over` one level up.

Associativity of the derived product is not needed either.
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.selfExtAlgebra` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.ExtAlgebraFromKInjective` is a sheaf of
graded `O_Y`-algebras on the Ext sheaves `Ext^n_{O_Y}(A, A)` of an *arbitrary* object of
`D(O_Y)`, global on `Y`, with no derived associativity involved: its multiplication is the
chain-level composition product of a K-injective representative. What separates that algebra from
the one computed here is the comparison of two models — the Koszul algebra is the composition
product of `SheafHom^•(K, K)` for a strictly perfect resolution `K` — and that comparison is
available, by the route the next three paragraphs describe. It factors both algebras through the
bimodule `H^*(SheafHom^•(K, I))`, and every chain-level input it needs is available in
general:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionMap` for arbitrary triples of complexes,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionMap_assoc` for four of them, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionMap_natural` in all three variables including
dinaturality in the middle one. The *cohomology*-level pairing for three distinct complexes is
available too: `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyComposition` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul` take three complexes `(K, L, M)` and land in
`H^{i+j}(SheafHom^•(K, M))`, with the former self case the diagonal instance `K = L = M = P`,
and `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul_assoc` is their associativity for four complexes — whose
off-diagonal instances are the two module laws making `H^*(SheafHom^•(K, I))` a bimodule over
`H^*(SheafHom^•(I, I))` and `H^*(SheafHom^•(K, K))`. So the bimodule can be spoken of, and
the comparison maps between the two models are available together with their multiplicativity:
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexCohomologyModelComparison` names
the class `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyClassOfHom` in `H⁰(SheafHom^•(K, I))` of an arbitrary
morphism of complexes `κ : K ⟶ I`, and proves that the two maps `κ` induces,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sourceComparison` on `SheafHom^•(I, I)` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.targetComparison` on `SheafHom^•(K, K)`, are *unital maps of modules* for
the pairing — `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMap_sourceComparison_homologyMul` on the left,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMap_targetComparison_homologyMul` on the right, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMap_sourceComparison_homologyOne` and
`..._targetComparison_homologyOne` saying that each sends its unit to the class of `κ`. Those are
the cohomology transports of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionMap_natural_source` and `..._natural_target`, hold for
an arbitrary `κ`, and are refined by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul_homologyClassOfHom` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyClassOfHom_homologyMul`, which identify each comparison map with
multiplication by the class of `κ` — on the right for precomposition, on the left for
postcomposition.

**That both comparison maps are quasi-isomorphisms needs no new mathematics: both halves are
already in the library.** For `SheafHom^•(I, I) ⟶ SheafHom^•(K, I)` it is Stacks 0A95,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homMap_quasiIso`, read with the identity of `I` in the covariant
variable, so only K-injectivity of `I` is used; for `SheafHom^•(K, K) ⟶ SheafHom^•(K, I)` it is
Stacks 08JH, `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homMap_quasiIso_of_isFiniteFreeSummand`, read at
`E = K`, so only strict perfection of `K` is used. Both are recorded as
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.quasiIso_sourceComparison` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.quasiIso_targetComparison`, with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_homologyMap_comparison` for the degreewise consequence.

**The model comparison.**
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.modelComparison` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexCohomologyModelIso` is a unital
homomorphism of sheaves of graded algebras from
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra Rc I` to
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra Rc K`, an isomorphism in every degree
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_modelComparison_app`), and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.extSelfComparisonIso` reads it out of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.extSelfGradedAlgebra`, whose pieces are the Ext sheaves themselves. The
morphism-of-graded-algebras API it uses is
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom`, with `UnitalHom.inverse` for a degreewise isomorphism.
Multiplicativity needed one further cohomology-level input,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul_assoc` read at the class of `κ`, recorded as
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul_homologyMap_targetComparison`.

**The descent of scalars.**
`Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.extSelfAlgebra` is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra` of the Koszul complex *descended* along
`O_X ⟶ O_X/I` by `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descend`, so `modelComparison`, which compares
the two algebras over `O_X`, has to be conjugated through that descent:
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.descend` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentHom` descends an arbitrary
unital homomorphism between two annihilated sheaves of graded algebras — by fullness of
restriction of scalars along a locally surjective morphism of sheaves of rings, Stacks 0930,
so no linearity is reproved — and
`Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.extSelfAlgebraModelIso` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulSelfExtAlgebraModelDescent` is the
descended comparison. Over the quotient, therefore,
`Stacks.RegularSequence.KoszulChainData.isIso_extComparisonOfKInjective` gives the conclusion
below for the model whose multiplication is the composition product of a *K-injective*
representative rather than of the Koszul complex.

What remains between this comparison and the tag is the gluing over a cover, described below.
Both multiplications identified by `modelComparison` are chain-level composition products, so
the *intrinsic* comparison -- the one built from the derived composition alone, as the tag's
statement intends -- is not constructed here. More precisely, the *composition morphisms* are
matched:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.strictlyPerfectHomComparison_derivedSheafExtComposition` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexCompositionOnExtSheaves`
identifies the composition morphism of derived internal Homs of Stacks 0A8V with the composition
morphism of internal Hom complexes of Stacks 0A91 on cohomology sheaves, with no hypothesis on
the three complexes. What is *not* matched is the other factor of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sheafExtProduct`: the pairing
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedCohomologyTensorProduct` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.DerivedCohomologyProduct`, which is formed
on K-flat representatives, is nowhere identified with the cycle-level
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul`.

**For the gluing: the composition product and slice restriction.** To feed
`IsExteriorAlgebra.isIso_desc_app_of_coversTop` one needs the slice restriction of the *global*
algebra to the members of the cover to be the local algebra there, and that means both the
pieces and the multiplication. Both halves are in the library, for an arbitrary ringed site:

* the pieces are `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafHomHomologyOverIso` of
  `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexHomologySliceRestriction`,
  natural in both variables by `sheafHomHomologyOverIso_natural`. It is *not*
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedSheafExtRestrictionIso`: the pieces of
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra` are the cohomology sheaves of the internal Hom
  complex, whereas `derivedSheafExtRestrictionIso` compares
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedSheafExt`, the cohomology sheaf of the *derived* internal Hom.
  The two objects agree for a suitable resolution but are not the same construction;
* the multiplication is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionMap_over` of
  `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.InternalHom.CompositionMapSliceRestriction`:
  restriction to a slice site carries the composition morphism of Stacks 0A91 to the composition
  morphism of the restrictions, as an identity of morphisms of complexes. Its content is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.compositionSection_sheafHomRestrictionComplexIso` of
  `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.InternalHom.CompositionSliceRestriction`, that
  the restriction of a composite of homogeneous local sections is the composite of their
  restrictions; the passage to morphisms of complexes uses
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.tensorTotalOverIso` and nothing else. Its image on cohomology
  sheaves is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyMap_compositionMap_over`.

So `compositionMap` is compatible with restriction to a slice site, and the conservativity
statement is available as well.

**The graded algebra of a slice site.** The restriction of the global algebra to a member of a
cover lives on the slice site `(Opens X).over U`, so `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyMul` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra`, defined for a ringed *space*, do not suffice to
speak of it.
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyGradedAlgebra` of
`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.InternalHom.CompositionCohomologyProduct` is the
composition product and its graded algebra for an arbitrary ringed site, with the same three laws
(`homologyMul_assoc`, `homologyMul_one_mul`, `homologyMul_mul_one`); none of the argument is
space-specific, and the space-level development is literally that one read at
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.ringedSite`, the identification being `rfl` in every case
(`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexCohomologyProductSite`).

**The comparison the gluing needs is a homomorphism of sheaves of graded algebras.**
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyGradedAlgebraOverHom` of
`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.InternalHom.CompositionAlgebraSliceRestriction` is a
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom` from `(homologyGradedAlgebra X P).over U` to
`homologyGradedAlgebra (X.over U) (restrictComplex X P U)`, degreewise
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafHomHomologyOverIso X U P P n` and therefore an isomorphism in
every degree (`isIso_homologyGradedAlgebraOverHom_app`). Its two laws are
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyMul_sheafHomHomologyOverIso` -- proved on classes of
cocycles as `..._of_cycles` and extended by lifting two cohomology sections to cocycles on a
common refinement of the slice site -- and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyOne_sheafHomHomologyOverIso`, whose content is that
restriction carries the identity section to the identity section
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.idSection_sheafHomRestrictionComplexIso`).

The transports:

* a description of `sheafHomHomologyOverIso` on a class of a cocycle is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.map_homologyπ_sheafHomHomologyOverIso`, with
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafHomHomologyOverIso_hom_app_homologyπ` on local sections and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafHomCyclesOverMap` the comparison on cycles it factors
  through, read inside the complex by `sheafHomCyclesOverMap_iCycles`. The input needed is
  entirely Mathlib-level: `Stacks.Homology.mapHomologyIso_homologyπ` of
  `StacksFrontier.Algebra.Homology.HomotopyCategory.ExactFunctorHomology` -- the compatibility of
  `ShortComplex.mapHomologyIso` with `ShortComplex.homologyπ` -- with
  `Stacks.Homology.exactHomologyIso_hom_app_homologyπ` and
  `Stacks.Homology.map_homologyπ_exactHomologyIso_inv_app` its forms on a complex;
* the restriction compatibility of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.tensorTotalIsoTensorComplex` is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.map_tensorTotalIsoTensorComplex_hom_tensorComplexOverIso` and
  its inverse form, in
  `StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.TensorComplex.TotalComplexRestriction`. It is a
  transposition of the definition of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.tensorTotalOverIso`, which is that very composite.

**The second transport is not needed for the multiplicativity.** Read on
*local sections* -- the shape `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.map_mul` asks for -- the
statement needs no comparison of the two total tensor products at all: each side is computed by
`homologyMul_app_homologyπ` and then by `iCycles_cycleComposition_pureSection` on its own site,
and what is left over is `compositionSection_sheafHomRestrictionComplexIso`. The tensor
comparison is what the *morphism*-level route, through
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyMap_compositionMap_over`, would need.

**The slice site and the open subspace are identified.**
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.isIso_desc_app_of_coversTop` asks for
`IsExteriorAlgebra (B.over (V i)) (genOver κ (V i))`, the restriction of the global algebra to
the *slice site* at `V i`. `homologyGradedAlgebraOverHom` carries that to the graded algebra of
the restricted complex on `X.over (V i)`, while the local statement proved below,
`forall_isIso_extComparison_of_isKoszulSequenceExactOn`, lives on the restricted ringed *space*
`X.restrict U`. The passage between the two categories of sheaves of modules is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceEquiv` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.QuasicoherentAlgebras`, which is Mathlib's
`TopologicalSpace.Opens.sheafOfModulesEquivOver` read at `ringSheaf X`, with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspace_eq_restrict` saying that its target really is
`X.restrict U.isOpenEmbedding`. That declaration is in Mathlib, and the library also uses it in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ExteriorPowerFiniteType`,
`...ExteriorPowerShortExact` and `...QuasicoherentAlgebras` itself; as the implementation notes
of `StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.Defs` say, restriction to the slice site
and restriction to the open subspace "are identified by `Opens.sheafOfModulesEquivOver`".

**The transport of a sheaf of graded algebras across that equivalence.**
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.pushforward` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedSitePushforward` pushes a sheaf of graded
algebras forward along any continuous functor of sites with any structure map -- it is the
construction of which `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.over` is the case `Over.forget U` with
structure map the identity, and every field is the original field read at the image object --
and `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceGradedAlgebra` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraOpenSubspace` is its instance at
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceSiteFunctor`, whose degreewise identification with
`openSubspaceEquiv` is `rfl`
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceGradedAlgebra_piece`). What that transport does *not* carry
is `IsExteriorAlgebra`, which quantifies over all graded algebras on its site; what it does
carry, in both directions, is invertibility of a morphism present on both sides, since the
pushforward is an equivalence and so reflects isomorphisms
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_openSubspaceUnitalHom_app_iff`). Invertibility is what the gluing
primitive `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.isIso_app_of_coversTop` consumes, so the
gluing does not need the universal property to cross.

**The internal-Hom comparison.** The
chain at a member `V` of the cover is: the global algebra restricted to the slice site, carried
by `homologyGradedAlgebraOverHom` to
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.homologyGradedAlgebra (X.over V) (restrictComplex X P V)`, carried
by `openSubspaceGradedAlgebra` to a sheaf of graded algebras on `Opens ↥V`. The last link is to
identify *that* with `Stacks.SheafOfModules.homologyGradedAlgebra` of the restricted complex on
`X.restrict V`, and it is not formal: the sections of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafHomComplex` over an object `W` are morphisms of complexes on
the slice at `W`, so pushing them forward along `openSubspaceSiteFunctor` compares morphisms on
`(Over V).over (openSubspaceSiteFunctor V |>.obj W)` with morphisms on `(Opens ↥V).over W` --
the *nested* slice equivalence, applied to internal Homs. That comparison is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sheafHomComplexOpenSubspaceIso` of
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.InternalHom.HomComplexAlgebraOpenSubspace`, natural in
both variables by `sheafHomComplexOpenSubspaceIso_natural`, whence
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceHomologyGradedAlgebraOfSpaceHom` there is the whole chain as
a single `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom`, an isomorphism in every degree by
`isIso_openSubspaceHomologyGradedAlgebraOfSpaceHom_app`. The statement in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SheafHomSlice` is a different
one: what is unformalized there is the one-slice *sheaf of modules* form
`SheafHom(F, G)|_U ≅ SheafHom_{O|_U}(F|_U, G|_U)` on `Over U`, and the gluing does not use it.
Between the local statements below and the global one, the commuting square of structure maps
and quotients is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspace_descent_square` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentOpenSubspace`, together with
the transport `Stacks.SheafOfModules.descendOpenSubspaceHom` it unlocks and the two directions
in which the universal property of an exterior algebra crosses it,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_descendOpenSubspace` and
`isExteriorAlgebra_of_descendOpenSubspace`. That file takes the quotient downstairs to *be* the
pushforward of the quotient upstairs, which is what makes the square degenerate, and a consumer
holding a quotient of the structure sheaf formed independently on `X.restrict U` — which is what
every local statement below does, its quotient being formed from
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sectionOnRestriction` of the generating sections —
has to identify the two.

That identification is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceRingQuotientIso` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.QuotientRingSheafOpenSubspace`: the quotient of the
structure sheaf of `X.restrict U` by a sheaf of ideals whose sections over an open `W ⊆ U` are
the sections of a sheaf of ideals of `O_X|_U` over the corresponding object of `Opens X / U` is
the pushforward of the slice quotient, compatibly with the two quotient maps. It is not
bookkeeping, a quotient being a sheafification and the pushforward of a sheafification being a
sheafification of nothing; what identifies the two is that both are locally surjective quotient
maps out of the same sheaf of rings with the same kernel, together with the fact — the
engine of this — that a quotient is determined by its kernel:
`Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheafIso` of
`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheafRecognition` recognises any locally
surjective morphism of sheaves of commutative rings out of `O` whose kernel over every object is
the ideal of sections of `I` there as `O/I`, and
`Stacks.Sites.pushforwardQuotientRingIso` of
`StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheafPushforward` is its form along a
continuous and cocontinuous functor of sites. The hypothesis that the two sheaves of ideals have
the same sections is the content and not a weakening — different ideals have different quotients —
and `Stacks.Sites.sectionIdeal_pushforwardKerIdeal` exhibits an ideal satisfying it.

Two steps above the quotient remain, and neither concerns the chosen
exterior algebra. The first of them — the transport of `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descend`
along an identification of the quotient it descends along, `descend` being taken along a *named*
morphism of sheaves of rings, so that an isomorphism of its targets does not by itself move the
descended algebra — is
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descendRingIsoHom` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraDescentQuotientIso`, with
`isExteriorAlgebra_descend_of_ringIso` carrying the universal property of an exterior algebra
across it. It rests on nothing geometric: restriction of scalars along a locally surjective
morphism of sheaves of rings is fully faithful
(`PresheafOfModules.restrictHomEquivOfIsLocallySurjective`), a unital homomorphism of sheaves of
graded algebras is a degreewise morphism plus two conditions on local sections, and the
bijection is the identity on local sections, so the universal property crosses with no transport
of its own (`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.of_scalarRestrict`). The second step is
in `StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.QuotientRingSheafOpenSubspaceIdeal`.
The ideals the statements below generate are `Stacks.Sites.idealOfHom`, local closures, so the
identification on sections is not objectwise: what crosses it is that local closure commutes with
the slice/open-subspace site equivalence. That is a statement about sieves —
`Stacks.Sites.functorPullback_mem_iff`, a sieve on an image object covering exactly when its
functor-pullback does, for any cover-preserving and cocontinuous functor of sites — read on
submodules as `Stacks.SheafOfModules.mem_localClosure_openSubspace_iff`, whence
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.sectionIdeal_idealOfHom_openSubspace` turns an objectwise agreement of two
images into the hypothesis `openSubspaceRingQuotientIso` consumes, and
`sectionIdeal_idealOfHom_openSubspaceEquiv` is its canonical case, the morphism downstairs being
the pushforward of the one upstairs.

The instantiation of the transport at the open-subspace spelling is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_descend_openSubspaceRingQuotient`, asking for one
annihilation hypothesis and not two. The obstruction it clears is that
`(ringSheaf X).over U` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf (X.sheaf.over U)` are one sheaf of
rings under two spellings — `sheafCompose` after `overPullback` and `overPullback` after
`sheafCompose` — that elaboration crosses and instance search does not. The resolution is to
route through one spelling: each sheaf of rings is named once as a reducible abbreviation whose
ascribed type pins the spelling, and the one instance that cannot be cited at the new spelling is
re-proved from `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isLocallySurjective_whisker`, which speaks only of the
underlying presheaf morphism.

**The objectwise comparison for the Koszul morphism needs no second free sheaf of modules.**
It is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sectionIdeal_idealOfHom_koszulHomOfSections` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulIdealOpenSubspace`, which supplies `hI'`
for the ideal the local statements below generate against
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sliceIdealOfSections`, the sheaf of ideals that
`f₁, …, f_r` generate on the slice site. Both sides are computed against one description:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.mem_range_app_free_iff` says the objectwise image of a morphism out of a
finite free sheaf of modules is the span of the images of the tautological sections, over an
arbitrary site, so each side is the ideal generated by the restrictions of `f₁, …, f_r` over the
open in question. Building `koszulHomOfSections` again on
the slice site and citing `sectionIdeal_idealOfHom_openSubspaceEquiv` is not needed: what both
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.mem_localClosure_openSubspace_iff` and `openSubspaceRingQuotientIso` ask of
the ideal upstairs is its *sections*, so the ideal there is written as the local closure of an
objectwise span (`Stacks.SheafOfModules.spanSections`) and no sheafified coproduct over the slice
topology ever enters. The transport in
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.sectionOnRestriction` is not the obstacle either:
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.map_sectionOnRestriction` discharges it by substituting
`U.isOpenEmbedding.functor.obj ⊤ = U`, after which two morphisms of `Opens X` with the same source
and target are equal. What does bite is spelling, and in exactly one place: the free sheaf of
modules must never be written at two spellings of its sheaf of rings, since
`(SheafOfModules.unit (CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat J O)).freeHomEquiv` applied to a morphism
typed through `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf` sends the elaborator into the coproduct and
past the heartbeat limit. The free-sheaf computation is therefore stated once, at the spelling
`koszulHomOfSections` produces, and the value of the Koszul morphism on a generator is read off
`Stacks.PresheafOfModules.sections_ext_top` rather than off its own definition.

With it,
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isExteriorAlgebra_descend_koszulOpenSubspace` is
`isExteriorAlgebra_descend_openSubspaceRingQuotient` with `hI'` discharged, so the whole passage
from the quotient formed on `X.restrict U` to the pushforward of the slice quotient is closed.
**What remains for `hBV` is the graded-algebra assembly above the quotient.** The chain is:
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.descendOverHom` to restrict the globally
descended algebra to the slice site,
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.sitePushforward` to carry that off the slice site,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.descendOpenSubspaceHom` to exchange pushforward and descent,
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.descend` of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceHomologyGradedAlgebraOfSpaceHom` to reach the
composition-product algebra of the restricted complex on `X.restrict U`,
`isExteriorAlgebra_descend_koszulOpenSubspace` to move to the quotient the local statements use,
and `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.isExteriorAlgebra_of_descendOver` with
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_of_descendOpenSubspace` to carry the universal property
back in the direction the gluing consumes. Each link is degreewise invertible, but degreewise
invertibility is **not** what `hBV` needs. `hBV` is a universal property, and a universal property
quantifies over all sheaves of graded algebras on its site, so a degreewise isomorphism carries it
only across a link that stays on one site. Every link above does stay on one site except the second:
the passage between the slice site `(Opens X).over U`, over which the gluing runs, and the open
subspace `Opens ↥U`, on which the local statements live.

**That passage is a theorem.**
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.of_sitePushforward` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedSitePushforwardEquivalence` carries the
universal property of a pushed-forward sheaf of graded algebras back to the original, and
`id_of_sitePushforward` is its form at the identity generator — the form in which the chain uses
it. What it needs is *not* essential surjectivity of `pushforward` on sheaves of graded
algebras, and no multiplication or unit is transported along a counit anywhere. The test algebra
of the property being *proved* travels forwards along the pushforward, so what is needed is
**fullness and faithfulness of `sitePushforward` on unital homomorphisms** —
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.exists_sitePushforward_eq` and
`UnitalHom.sitePushforward_injective` — together with essential surjectivity of the functor of
*sites*, which is where fullness propagates the two laws of a homomorphism off the objects in
the image of that functor, by naturality (`map_mul_of_iso`, `map_one_of_hom`,
`SquareZero.of_iso`).

For an equivalence of sites, continuous in both directions, all three hypotheses are free:
`Stacks.Sites.pushforwardModuleEquivOfSiteEquiv` is the pushforward of sheaves of modules along
such an equivalence, with no change of scalars, as an equivalence of categories, for an
**arbitrary** sheaf of rings on the target site. That generality is what the chain actually
needs and what `Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceEquiv` alone does not give: the algebra
crossing the site at the second link is descended along a *quotient* of the structure sheaf, so
the pushforward in play is the one at `openSubspaceRingSheaf` and not at the structure sheaf.
The instances are `Stacks.SheafOfModules.full_pushforward_openSubspaceRingSheaf` and
`faithful_pushforward_openSubspaceRingSheaf`; the instance at the structure sheaf, which the
other direction of the chain uses, is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_id_of_openSubspaceGradedAlgebra`.

The chain's links are therefore assembled: `isExteriorAlgebra_id_descend_of_openSubspace` is the
site change together with the descent interchange, and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_id_over_descend_of_openSubspace` is all three links of
the descent presentation at once, asking for the global annihilation hypothesis only. What
`hBV` needs beyond these is the three Koszul-specific steps — the passage through
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.openSubspaceHomologyGradedAlgebraOfSpaceHom`,
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isExteriorAlgebra_descend_koszulOpenSubspace`, and
the comparison over `U` of the restriction of the global resolution with the Koszul complex —
all of which stay on one site, so each is one application of
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.id_ofDegreewiseIso`. No statement about
presentations of a site is left.

`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.isIso_desc_app_of_coversTop_of_hom` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ExteriorAlgebraSliceComparisonGluing` is the
cheaper hypothesis for the gluing: it replaces `hBV` by the existence of **some** degreewise
invertible homomorphism of the restrictions carrying one restricted generator to the other,
which is a statement about a morphism and needs no universal property to cross anything.

**Every link of the chain that stays on one site is a single application of one lemma.**
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsExteriorAlgebra.id_ofDegreewiseIso` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.GradedAlgebraIdentityGenerator` transports the
universal property at the identity generator along a degreewise invertible homomorphism, in
either direction by `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.UnitalHom.isIso_inverse_app`; since neither the
source nor the target module appears in the statement, a chain composes with no bookkeeping
about generators at all. The two links of the descent presentation are read off it as
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.isExteriorAlgebra_id_over_descend` and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isExteriorAlgebra_id_sitePushforward_descend`, and the annihilation
hypothesis that descent over a slice site consumes is
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.smul_over_eq_zero`, the global one read at the underlying object.
What the assembly must also supply is the one input that is mathematics and not plumbing: the
complex whose composition-product algebra is restricted is a *global* resolution, and over `U` the
local statements speak of the *Koszul* complex, so the two models must be compared there by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.modelComparison` at a morphism from the Koszul complex to the restriction
of the global one. Both hypotheses of that comparison are available: the restriction of a
K-injective complex to an open is K-injective
(`Stacks.Cohomology.Localization.isKInjective_over`) and the Koszul complex is strictly perfect.

**The Ext sheaf meant throughout is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedSheafExt` of
`Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/InternalHom/DerivedSheafExt.lean`**, the cohomology sheaf of the
derived internal Hom with its degree argument first. A second declaration of that name, with the
degree last, lives in `Topology/Sheaves/Cohomology/InternalHom/ExtSheaves.lean` and is not the
one the Koszul development uses.

